## Phase 3 — Top-down macro sensitivity
- Rolling regressions (63d, 126d, 252d) of each metal's returns on factor changes.
  Betas, t-stats, R², and beta stability charts.
- Specific study: gold vs real yields over time. Quantify the breakdown of the
  relationship and test what explains the residual (dollar, central bank buying,
  geopolitical risk proxies, ETF flows).
- Copper vs China: relationship with China PMI, CNH, China data surprises, and
  Chinese property equities.
- Event studies: US CPI, NFP, FOMC, plus China data releases. Forward returns at
  t+1, t+5, t+20 by surprise sign and size. Report hit rate, mean, median, payoff
  ratio and N for every result.
- Regime tags: inflation regime, Fed cycle, dollar trend (DXY vs 200d MA), vol regime
  (VIX and MOVE percentiles), China policy stance (manual config). Condition all
  results on regime.
- Daily shock classifier: label each day by sign of 10y yield change and SPX change,
  split into real yield vs breakeven driven. Report each metal's behaviour per regime.

## Phase 4 — Bottom-up fundamentals and flow of funds
- Physical tightness dashboard: LME, COMEX and SHFE inventories (look up tickers),
  LME cash-to-3M spreads, combined visible stocks in days of consumption.
- Positioning and flows: CFTC managed money net positioning (gold, silver, copper),
  gold and silver ETF holdings, central bank gold purchases (likely a manual quarterly
  input file). Z-scores and extremes.
- Copper supply/demand balance sheet: supply (mine production, refined production,
  scrap), demand (China apparent consumption, rest of world, end-use split),
  implied balance, and change in inventories. Much of this data is not on Bloomberg:
  build clean CSV/Excel input templates with source and publication date columns,
  and validate inputs on load.
- Make the balance sheet scenario-driven: e.g. "mine disruption rate +2pp",
  "China grid investment −10%". Output the implied balance change.
- Confirmation table: for each metal, does fundamentals/flow agree or disagree with
  the macro signal? Disagreements are flagged, not averaged away.

